# Trivy ignore file for known upstream vulnerabilities
# These CVEs exist in upstream base images that we cannot directly fix.
# We document them here and track upstream fixes.
#
# Format: CVE-ID  # reason
# See: https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy/latest/docs/configuration/filtering/

# CVE-2025-44005: Step CA Has Authorization Bypass in ACME and SCEP
# Affects: smallstep/certificates < v0.29.0
# Status: Fixed in our step-ca image via PR #189 (bump to v0.29.0)
# Impact: Other images (alertmanager, caddy, grafana, loki, mosquitto, prometheus)
#         include this library through their upstream base images.
#         We cannot fix this until upstream releases new versions.
# Mitigation: Only step-ca uses ACME functionality in our deployment.
#             The vulnerability is an authorization bypass, not RCE.
# Track: https://github.com/smallstep/certificates/releases
CVE-2025-44005

# CVE-2024-24790: golang: net/netip: Unexpected behavior from Is methods for IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses
# Affects: Go stdlib < 1.21.11 / 1.22.4
# Status: Upstream issue - Atlantis bundles a terraform1.8.5 binary built with Go 1.22.1
# Impact: Only the bundled terraform1.8.5 binary is affected (other bundled terraform
#         versions and the Atlantis binary itself are built with newer Go).
#         We cannot fix this until Atlantis upstream rebuilds that binary with newer Go.
# Mitigation: This is an edge case in IPv6 handling, low practical impact
#             for Atlantis which primarily handles Terraform/OpenTofu operations.
# Track: https://github.com/runatlantis/atlantis/releases
CVE-2024-24790

# CVE-2025-68121: crypto/tls: Incorrect certificate validation during TLS session resumption
# Affects: Go stdlib < 1.24.13 / 1.25.7 / 1.26.0-rc.3
# Status: Upstream issue - as of Atlantis v0.47.0 (latest release), the Atlantis binary
#         itself and its bundled terraform1.8.5/1.9.8/1.10.5 and conftest/git-lfs
#         binaries are all still built with older Go toolchains.
# Impact: Atlantis image contains multiple Go binaries compiled with vulnerable
#         crypto/tls. We cannot fix this until Atlantis upstream rebuilds with a
#         patched Go toolchain - no newer release is available yet.
# Mitigation: Exploiting this requires a malicious TLS server the client resumes a
#             session against; Atlantis's outbound TLS clients (VCS API, Terraform
#             registry) talk to trusted, pinned hosts, not attacker-controlled servers.
# Track: https://github.com/runatlantis/atlantis/releases
CVE-2025-68121

# CVE-2026-33186: google.golang.org/grpc/authz: Authorization bypass due to improper
#                 HTTP/2 path validation
# Affects: google.golang.org/grpc < v1.79.3
# Status: Upstream issue - as of Atlantis v0.47.0 (latest release), the bundled
#         conftest and terraform binaries still vendor an older grpc-go.
# Impact: Bundled conftest/terraform binaries include a vulnerable grpc-go dependency.
#         We cannot fix this until those upstream projects rebuild with grpc-go >= 1.79.3.
# Mitigation: These binaries are invoked as local CLI tools by Atlantis, not run as
#             gRPC servers accepting external connections - the authz bypass requires
#             gRPC's HTTP/2-based authorization interceptor to be in use as a server.
# Track: https://github.com/runatlantis/atlantis/releases
CVE-2026-33186
